commit 444fc668529a8b89ffcd7e584c242c76e5370cee
Author: Jerry Xu <mxudev@gmail.com>
Date:   Fri Oct 16 13:51:41 2026 +0200

    Kernel calls keep their memory's address instead of asking the store
    Since step 7 each of p50's 6,058 kernel instances has a store of its
    own, and every kernel call asked that store for its memory twice. Those
    lookups miss the cache now (the profile: about +188 us of host time a
    512-frame callback). Each instance now keeps its memory's address and
    size from its build, and a call slices them through `&mut self`, so
    nothing else can reach the memory meanwhile. This is sound because a
    kernel memory never moves in either layout: P2 refuses to move one and
    P1 reserves 4 GiB against the 32 MiB cap. The engine field is now
    crate-private, so no other engine can be put in a PackageSet.
    Building checks the address after each kernel's first reset, and debug
    builds at every call. New test a_kernel_memory_never_moves (RENDER-32):
    a kernel that grows by a page every call up to the cap plays its input
    bit for bit, under P2 and P1. Under a layout that may move, it and 32
    other runtime tests fail. No sound changes: 8 of 8 renders, the p16
    render ebc87e0b..., and the five recorded tests pass.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01R1SbPDdWrZoQ7z8dxU28hJ

