 **RENDER-32.** Store layout: one wasmtime `Store` per kernel instance. Each kernel memory reserves 32 MiB with a 64 KiB guard, keeps no room to grow and never moves, so every access is checked against a fixed bound, and Cranelift's Spectre guard on those checks is off. That guard goes back on, or the layout is measured again, before a kernel from a package other than std runs ([Deferred](#deferred-with-triggers)). Never a `Store` shared across plans, which keeps every replaced instance's memory until it drops. G7f judges a layout against S, the commit before step 7 (one `Store` per renderer, no arena), measured fresh in every run: S plays in a process of its own, taking turns with the app over the same music, in 5 rounds. By the mean, a p50 block MUST cost at most 80 µs more than S's (3% of a 2.67 ms block), and a 512-frame callback at most 320 µs more than S's (3% of a callback). Against S, p50's footprint MUST grow by at most 64 MB and its build by at most half, and two p50 renderers MUST build side by side within 25% of the process's address space. An activation's block MUST take under half the device period (5.3 ms at 512 frames). The layout and the bounds are the owner's (2026-10-15; design-7 §11.3).
 - *Why:* per-instance stores make instances movable between plans, and free a replaced instance's memory when it drops. wasmtime's default reserves 4 GiB a memory, so only about 5 copies of p50 fit in one process, and an export holds two. Kernels import nothing, so they have no clock to time a speculative read with, and the guard cost 6% to 10% of block time (Linux, the Mac). The mean is judged, not the p99: even paired, the p99's difference moves by hundreds of µs a block from one pair to the next, which 5 pairs can't settle, while the mean's stays within about 12 µs.
